WEBVTT

00:00:00.430 --> 00:00:05.000
Broken access control is one of the most important risks in Node.js APIs and web

00:00:05.000 --> 00:00:05.900
applications.

00:00:05.900 --> 00:00:10.480
A route existing behind authentication does not automatically mean every object, action,

00:00:10.480 --> 00:00:11.795
or field is authorized.

00:00:11.795 --> 00:00:16.430
Object-level authorization asks whether this caller may access this specific record.

00:00:16.430 --> 00:00:20.290
Function-level authorization asks whether this caller may perform this action.

00:00:20.290 --> 00:00:24.500
Field-level decisions may also matter when APIs expose sensitive attributes.

00:00:24.500 --> 00:00:28.780
Injection risks appear when untrusted input influences commands, queries, templates,

00:00:28.780 --> 00:00:31.060
expressions, or downstream interpreters.

00:00:31.060 --> 00:00:35.400
The defensive pattern is to avoid string-built commands or queries, use safe APIs,

00:00:35.400 --> 00:00:38.950
validate inputs, encode outputs, and keep privileges limited.

00:00:38.950 --> 00:00:43.490
Server-rendered pages and API-backed applications also need to handle XSS risk by

00:00:43.490 --> 00:00:48.490
controlling how untrusted content becomes HTML, JavaScript, URLs, or attributes.

00:00:48.490 --> 00:00:52.360
CSRF matters when browser cookies are used for authentication and requests can be

00:00:52.360 --> 00:00:53.795
triggered from another site.

00:00:53.795 --> 00:00:57.320
CORS is often misunderstood; it is a browser access control mechanism,

00:00:57.320 --> 00:00:59.978
not a replacement for authentication or authorization.

00:00:59.978 --> 00:01:04.090
A permissive CORS policy can create unintended browser-based access patterns,

00:01:04.090 --> 00:01:07.300
especially when combined with credentials or sensitive responses.

00:01:07.300 --> 00:01:11.300
Security headers, rate limiting, and careful response design all support defense in depth.

00:01:11.300 --> 00:01:15.560
APIs should avoid excessive data exposure by returning only what the caller needs and is

00:01:15.560 --> 00:01:19.680
authorized to see. Most importantly, authorization must be enforced on the server.

00:01:19.680 --> 00:01:21.650
Hidden UI buttons, disabled form fields,

00:01:21.650 --> 00:01:24.300
or client-side route guards are useful for experience,

00:01:24.300 --> 00:01:25.980
but they are not security boundaries.
